  "slug": "phylogenomics-australian-76a6",
  "summary": "Genome-wide datasets now test whether gene tree discordance reflects incomplete lineage sorting, introgression, or insufficient resolution.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints and one peer-reviewed study, genome-scale data are used to resolve relationships where gene trees and organellar or nuclear signals disagree. In Australian Gossypium, whole-genome phylogenomics finds plastid-nuclear discordance but little nuclear evidence of introgression, which revisits homoploid hybrid speciation. In Dipsacales, whole-genome resequencing of 88 species resolves Linnina backbone relationships and attributes discordance to incomplete lineage sorting (ILS) and possible ancient gene flow. In Aizoaceae, phylotranscriptomics shows that gene-dense data resolve major relationships, while short internodes limit resolution within the rapid radiation. Flatfish whole-genome phylogenomics and synteny support a single origin of body-plan asymmetry. What is new is the emphasis on data density and genomic context, with the ice plant result marking limits on what gene-dense data can resolve.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
